{"subjectId":"python","id":"mocking","title":"Mocking & Test Doubles","group":"Testing & Tooling","order":62,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A test double stands in for a slow, flaky or external dependency — an API, the clock, the database — so a unit test checks only <em>your</em> code. <code>unittest.mock</code> is the standard toolkit.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-category\"></i>Kinds of test doubles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Double</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stub</td><td>Returns canned answers</td><td><code>get_rate()</code> always returns <code>83.0</code></td></tr><tr><td>Mock</td><td>Records calls so you can assert on them</td><td>“<code>send_email</code> was called once with this address”</td></tr><tr><td>Fake</td><td>A working lightweight implementation</td><td>An in-memory dict instead of a real database</td></tr><tr><td>Spy</td><td>Wraps the real object and records calls</td><td><code>Mock(wraps=real_func)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-masks-theater\"></i>Mock basics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from unittest.mock import Mock\n\napi = Mock()\napi.get_user.return_value = {\"id\": 7, \"name\": \"Asha\"}   # stub a method\n\nprint(api.get_user(7)[\"name\"])                    # Asha\nprint(api.get_user.call_count)                    # 1\nprint(api.get_user.call_args)                     # call(7)\napi.get_user.assert_called_once_with(7)           # passes silently\n\nprint(type(api.anything.you.like).__name__)       # Mock   ← attributes are created on demand</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>side_effect: exceptions and sequences</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from unittest.mock import Mock\n\ndef fetch_with_retry(client, attempts=3):\n    for i in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            return client.fetch()\n        except ConnectionError:\n            if i == attempts - 1:\n                raise\n\nclient = Mock()\nclient.fetch.side_effect = [ConnectionError(), ConnectionError(), \"data\"]   # fail, fail, succeed\nprint(fetch_with_retry(client), client.fetch.call_count)   # data 3\n\nclient.fetch.side_effect = ConnectionError(\"down\")          # always fail\ntry:\n    fetch_with_retry(client)\nexcept ConnectionError as e:\n    print(\"gave up:\", e)                                     # gave up: down\n\ndouble = Mock(side_effect=lambda x: x * 2)                   # compute the return value\nprint(double(21))                                            # 42</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-replace\"></i>patch: replace a name during a test</h3>\n<p><code>patch</code> swaps an attribute for a mock and restores it afterwards. Patch <strong>where the name is looked up</strong>, not where it was defined: if <code>app.py</code> does <code>from time import time</code>, patch <code>app.time</code>, not <code>time.time</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import time\nfrom unittest.mock import patch\n\nclass Greeter:\n    def greeting(self):\n        hour = time.localtime().tm_hour\n        return \"Good morning\" if hour &lt; 12 else \"Good afternoon\"\n\nfake = time.struct_time((2026, 9, 29, 9, 0, 0, 1, 272, 0))\nwith patch.object(time, \"localtime\", return_value=fake):      # context-manager form\n    print(Greeter().greeting())                                # Good morning\n\n@patch.object(Greeter, \"greeting\", return_value=\"Hi\")          # decorator form\ndef check(mock_greeting):\n    result = Greeter().greeting()\n    mock_greeting.assert_called_once()\n    return result\n\nprint(check())                                                 # Hi\nprint(time.localtime is not None)                              # True   ← original restored</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import os\nfrom unittest.mock import patch, mock_open\n\nwith patch.dict(os.environ, {\"FEATURE_X\": \"on\"}):     # temporary environment\n    print(os.environ[\"FEATURE_X\"])                    # on\nprint(\"FEATURE_X\" in os.environ)                      # False\n\nwith patch(\"builtins.open\", mock_open(read_data=\"line1\\nline2\")):\n    with open(\"any/path.txt\") as f:                   # no real file touched\n        print(f.read().splitlines())                  # ['line1', 'line2']</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield-check\"></i>spec &amp; autospec: mocks that fail like the real thing</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from unittest.mock import create_autospec\n\nclass PaymentGateway:\n    def charge(self, amount: int, currency: str) -&gt; str:\n        raise NotImplementedError\n\ngateway = create_autospec(PaymentGateway, instance=True)\ngateway.charge.return_value = \"txn-1\"\nprint(gateway.charge(500, \"INR\"))                     # txn-1\n\ntry:\n    gateway.charge(500)                               # wrong signature → error, like the real method\nexcept TypeError:\n    print(\"signature checked\")                        # signature checked\ntry:\n    gateway.refund(1)                                 # no such method on the real class\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"unknown method rejected\")                  # unknown method rejected</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A plain <code>Mock()</code> accepts any call and any attribute, so a typo like <code>mock.assert_caled_once()</code> or a renamed method can make a broken test pass. Use <code>spec=</code>/<code>autospec</code> (and Python rejects <code>assert_*</code> typos since 3.12).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-flask\"></i>pytest's monkeypatch</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def test_uses_api_key(monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setenv(\"API_KEY\", \"test-key\")\n    monkeypatch.setattr(\"shop.payments.charge\", lambda amount: \"ok\")\n    assert checkout(100) == \"ok\"          # undone automatically after the test</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Mock at the boundaries: network, time, randomness, file system, third-party services.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Mocking your own internal functions everywhere — tests then mirror the implementation and break on every refactor while missing real bugs.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why do you patch where an object is used, not where it's defined?</summary><div><p><code>from module import name</code> copies the reference into the importing module's namespace. Patching the original module doesn't change that copy, so the code under test still calls the real function. Patch <code>consumer_module.name</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Mock vs MagicMock?</summary><div><p><code>MagicMock</code> is a <code>Mock</code> that also pre-configures magic methods (<code>__len__</code>, <code>__iter__</code>, <code>__enter__</code>, …), so it can stand in for containers or context managers. <code>patch</code> uses <code>MagicMock</code> by default.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is side_effect used for?</summary><div><p>To make a mock raise an exception, return successive values from an iterable, or compute its result with a function — for testing error handling and retries.</p></div></details></section>"}